[h2]Dying now costs exactly half what it did[/h2]
[list]
[*]A player asked to cut the death penalty by another 50 percent from where it already was. This mod had already reduced the on-death experience loss to 10 percent of the original game's. That reduction is now [b]halved again[/b], so every death costs exactly half of what it cost in the previous version, on every difficulty, at every level. Measured against the original game, the penalty is now 5 percent of vanilla.
[*][b]The high-level ceiling moved with it, and it had to.[/b] The penalty grows with the cube of your character level, and this mod raises the level cap to 1000, so above roughly level 87 on Legendary you were sitting on a flat ceiling rather than on the formula. Halving the formula alone would have left that ceiling in place and you would have felt no change at all at exactly the levels the complaint was about. Both moved together: the formula divisor and the cap.
[*]What that looks like in practice: level 55 Legendary was 12,940 XP and is now [b]6,470[/b]. Level 85 Normal was 6,824 and is now [b]3,412[/b]. Level 85 Legendary was 47,765 and is now [b]23,883[/b]. Level 100 Legendary was the 50,000 ceiling and is now [b]25,000[/b].
[/list]

[h2]The tombstone still returns 100 percent of what you lost[/h2]
[list]
[*]An earlier ruling made the death marker's experience recovery equal to the experience the death took, rather than a fixed fraction of it. That was built as an [b]equality[/b] on purpose, so it survives retunes like this one. The game hands your grave the amount it actually took from you, so when the penalty halved, the recovery halved with it [b]automatically, with no change to the recovery side at all[/b].
[*]Concretely, at level 85 Legendary you lose 23,883 XP and the marker gives back 23,883 XP. Not half of it, and not more than it. Had the recovery been hardcoded as a percentage back then, this update would have quietly turned dying and walking back into a way to gain experience. It cannot.
[/list]

[i]Scope, measured rather than asserted: this update changes [b]two fields on one database record[/b], the game engine record the game actually loads. Nothing else in 51,331 records moved, no new records were added or removed, and no item, monster, drop rate, chest, quest, area or map data changed. The world map, quest archive, text archive and creature archive are byte for byte identical to the previous update. Experience gain, the level table and the level cap are all asserted untouched by their own gates.[/i]

[i]Not yet confirmed in play: the arithmetic is proved over 3,012 checked points and the marker equality is derived from the game's own code, but nobody has died in-game on this build to watch the two numbers match on screen.[/i]

